### Data visualization with Matplotlib

1. Figure and Axes
    Figure is the overall canvas
    Axes are plotting areas inside the Figure

2. Basic plot types
    Line plot - line diagram over time or sequence
    Scatter plot - a dot for each data pair (two variables)
        Best for relationship between two continuous variables
    Histogram - distribution of a single variable
        Best for continuous variables 
    Bar plot - Categorical comparisions
        Best for categorical summaries eg Status counts

3. Customization
    Titles, axis labels, legends
    Colors, markers, line styles
    Gridlines, annotations

4. Workflow
    Import Matplotlib (import matplotlib.pyplot as plt)
    Create figure and axes (plt.figure(), plt.subplots())
    Plot data (plt.plot(), plt.hist(), etc)
    Customize (plt.title(), plt.xlabel(), etc)
    Show or save (plt.show(). plt.savefig())

We will practice with Breast Cancer Wisconsin Dataset
    source: UCI ML Repo. also available directly in sklearn.datasets
    rows: 569
    column: 30 features + 1 target
    Target: benign/malignant
    Features: mean radius, texture, perimeter, area, smoothness, compactness, concaavity, symmetry, fractal dimension etc 

Seaborn Pairplot:
    It creates a grid of scatter plots for every pair of selected features
    Adds Histogram or (KDEs) on the diagonal to show distributions
    Colors points by categorical variable (here benign vs malignant)
    Helps spot correlation, separations, and clusters across many features simultaneously

correlation matrix + heatmap
    Pairplot are great visually but can get overwhelming with too many features 
    Option is: correlation matrix + heatmap

Boxplot:
    Shows median, IQR, and outliers
    Great for quick comparisons 

Violin plot:
    Adds a kernel density estimate (like a smoothed histogram) around the boxplot.
    Great for visualizing distribution shape